1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:10,900
So one of my favorite authors is Mark Twain.

3
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,620
You know, good old Samuel Langhorne Clements.

4
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:16,700
He was kind of cross-grained and contrarian.

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:21,300
So I thought, hey, wouldn't it be interesting to see if he had anything to say about the scout law?

6
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,820
Now, yeah, he was never a Boy Scout.

7
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:27,880
But, you know, he had a way with words.

8
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:34,019
And I thought if I could find a Mark Twain quote for every point of the scout law,

9
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:35,379
it would probably be pretty funny.

10
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:37,480
And it would probably be pretty poignant.

11
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:41,000
Here's one that I found about being trustworthy.

12
00:00:42,379 --> 00:00:45,180
I am different from George Washington, he said.

13
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:48,760
I have a higher, grander standard of principle.

14
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,620
Washington could not lie.

15
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:56,040
I can lie, but I won't.

16
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,260
So who are you more like?

17
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,080
Are you more like George Washington or Mark Twain?

18
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,160
I'm a lot more like Mark Twain.

19
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:06,240
Right.

20
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:06,940
Yeah.

21
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:09,700
Hey, this is podcast number 20.

22
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:11,080
Oh, number 20.

23
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:37,240
Hi, welcome back to the Scout Masters podcast.

24
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:39,080
This is Clark Green.

25
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:46,200
In this edition, we're going to listen to an interview that I recently conducted with author David C. Scott,

26
00:01:46,539 --> 00:01:51,000
whose new book, The Scouting Party, is due to be released very soon.

27
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:55,280
And I think you'll find The Scouting Party a very worthy read.

28
00:01:55,759 --> 00:01:59,259
But let's go ahead and listen to the interview, and I'll be back with a couple of comments.

29
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:24,700
Today I am speaking with historian, author, and scouter, David C. Scott,

30
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:32,179
who has a new book that will be available the end of this month, June of 2010, called The Scouting Party,

31
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:39,140
subtitled Pioneering and Preservation, Progressivism, and Preparedness in the Making of the Boy Scouts of America.

32
00:02:39,339 --> 00:02:40,140
Good afternoon, David.

33
00:02:40,179 --> 00:02:40,660
How are you?

34
00:02:40,959 --> 00:02:41,519
Hello, Clark.

35
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:42,160
Doing great.

36
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:54,640
You were kind enough to send me a review copy, and I devoured it, and it is a really interesting and thorough take on the –

37
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:57,880
it's about the first 10 years of the scouting movement, is it not?

38
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,460
I would say that that's a fair assessment, yes.

39
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:07,400
I took a lot of time to put as much as I could into those first 10 years, the first decade,

40
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:12,780
since they were the – that was the most important in terms of criticality, if you will,

41
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:15,140
on the formation of the Boy Scouts of America.

42
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:20,040
And the party we're talking about in the title, the scouting party, is Ernest Thompson-Seaton,

43
00:03:20,359 --> 00:03:22,800
Baden Powell, and Dan Beard.

44
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:28,400
This is a study of their relationship as three founders of scouting.

45
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:33,280
There are different influences and everything, and it wasn't always an easy relationship between these three, was it?

46
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,380
No, there are three massive egos, and I'm sure that a couple of them wouldn't have made it out of a room,

47
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:46,020
but they were all three put together in the same room and started their negotiations of how to make it work.

48
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:52,580
So it was very rare that all three of them were seen together, once in 1910 and once in 1912.

49
00:03:52,580 --> 00:03:53,720
But that was about the extent of it.

50
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:58,820
And, yes, photographs were taken, so we do have documentation that they were together, and they all three survived.

51
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:04,720
But it was – it had to have been quite a meeting of the minds when they were together.

52
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:11,740
What I found really fascinating was – and I think anybody who's been around a little bit in scouting

53
00:04:11,740 --> 00:04:18,360
and has worked in a troop or a pack or a crew and worked in a district or council-level position

54
00:04:18,360 --> 00:04:23,480
understands that it – like you said, it takes a massive ego sometimes to make things go,

55
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:28,020
and they don't always get along, but the product is really what we keep our eyes on.

56
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:34,540
I was fascinated to learn that Ernest Thompson-Seaton seems to be the guy with the original idea.

57
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:35,360
Well, he was.

58
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:49,800
And when he met with Robert Baden-Pohl in October of 1906, BP was finishing off the creation of what he would eventually call his Boy Scouts,

59
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:52,860
but he didn't have all of the framework set down.

60
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:58,920
And when he did speak with Seaton at that meeting on October 30th, the pieces were finally put into place,

61
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:05,120
and BP was able to finalize his plan in several pamphlets that he published in 1907,

62
00:05:05,860 --> 00:05:12,680
actually published two of the three, Boy Scouting a suggestion, and they talked about Brown Sea and his plan.

63
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:18,040
And that's what finally gelled into what we know now as the Boy Scouting movement,

64
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:24,260
which I consider being born, really philosophically born on October 30th, 1906,

65
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:29,800
but in reality officially born of January 15th, 1908,

66
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:37,600
dating to the publication of BP's seminal work, the first fortnightly issuance of Scouting for Boys.

67
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:49,640
The interesting thing is that when Baden-Pohl sat down to do this, he had already seen Ernest Thompson Seaton's material about the Woodcraft Indians.

68
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:57,140
Well, he had, in fact, when Baden-Pohl was – and I say Baden-Pohl because that's the way he pronounced his name.

69
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:01,500
I know that it's going against the grain, but I have a bad habit of doing that.

70
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:09,800
But anyway, that being said, BP was vacationing at the house of his publisher, Pearson.

71
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:11,600
I can't recall his first name.

72
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:12,980
See, Arthur Pearson.

73
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,800
At his house in Surrey, England in the summer of 1906,

74
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:23,720
and this package arrived, this parcel arrived, and it was shipped by Ernest Seaton to BP

75
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:32,200
because Seaton wanted BP's opinion on how to work with him in terms of broadcasting Seaton's Woodcraft Indians.

76
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:34,740
And the book was the birchbark role of the Woodcraft Indians.

77
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:43,620
And that would be the book which would help provide the final piece for BP to help create his Boy Scouting scheme, as he called it.

78
00:06:44,020 --> 00:06:45,540
So that package arrived.

79
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:46,940
An unexpected package, mind you.

80
00:06:47,600 --> 00:06:52,840
But it arrived nonetheless, and that's when the gelling began.

81
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:59,500
To kind of fill people in about Ernest Thompson Seaton, tell us just a little bit about his history.

82
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:11,900
Well, Ernest Seaton was a Scots-born Canadian, and he came to the United States in the late 1890s to eventually get married, essentially is what it was.

83
00:07:11,900 --> 00:07:23,220
But he and his wife, her maiden name being Gallatin, her name is Grace Gallatin, they got married and moved to New York in the late 1890s.

84
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:30,420
And Seaton published a book of his animal stories called Wild Animals I Have Known.

85
00:07:30,420 --> 00:07:42,920
And in that story was – or in that book was a story that was based upon his experience with a wolf in the New Mexico lands, a desert out there named Lobo.

86
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:56,600
And the popularity of Lobo's story, the King Wolf of the Karumpa, that skyrocketed his fame through this book because he had already originally published the story of Lobo in Scribner's magazine.

87
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:58,820
And so people knew about Lobo.

88
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:02,940
Well, they wanted to hear more about these animal stories or these animals that he had come across.

89
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:10,760
So what he did was he created a genre of – a type of story to which gave human-like personalities and characteristics to the animals.

90
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:14,120
And so these stories were incredibly popular.

91
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:23,480
And in fact, he made a contract with his publisher, Scribner, that he would take no royalties on the first 2,000 copies of his book because he – they didn't think they would sell.

92
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:24,940
Well, he knew that it would.

93
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:30,640
So – but for every copy above 2,000, he would take a double royalty in a sense 20 percent.

94
00:08:31,940 --> 00:08:36,919
Well, after – see, that was done around March or April of 1898.

95
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:43,480
And by December, they had gone through multiple printings, and he was up about – in today's dollars, about $4 million.

96
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:49,480
So he was – about 200,000 in royalties, but about $4 million today.

97
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:51,300
So he was quite a wealthy guy.

98
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:52,960
He could do whatever he wanted to at that point.

99
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,940
And the scouting thing appealed to him.

100
00:08:56,540 --> 00:09:05,360
His approach to nature study was based a lot on Native American spirituality that he had picked up in his travels.

101
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:06,000
Absolutely.

102
00:09:06,180 --> 00:09:13,240
He was more concerned with the great spirit as opposed to classic traditional Christianity.

103
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:26,380
And he believed that the Native American – or as he referred to them, of course, the Red Man – the Native American was a superior human being in terms of their spirituality because they lived off the land.

104
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:29,420
And they did not – they did not desecrate it.

105
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:30,440
They didn't destroy it.

106
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:33,680
They didn't harm it in so much as they could.

107
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:38,040
So they lived and used the bounty of Mother Nature.

108
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:39,680
And they respected her.

109
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:48,920
That was his model for the way an American boy should grow up, being more like in the model of Tecumseh than anyone else.

110
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:53,219
I think that was kind of a minority opinion at the time.

111
00:09:53,699 --> 00:09:55,459
Well, it would have to have been a minority opinion.

112
00:09:55,599 --> 00:09:56,699
Most definitely, yes.

113
00:09:57,219 --> 00:10:05,360
Seton was – he was a bit of a visionary as compared to his compatriots Powell and Beard.

114
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:21,120
If you look at Baden-Pol and his work on scouting for boys, he considered the backwoodsmen or the knights of the old chivalric years, knights of King Arthur, as his model for the scouts.

115
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:34,440
Well, Daniel Carter Beard, Uncle Dan, Dan Beard as we call him, he looked at it as the pioneer, the plainsman, the buckskin man as the model for the American boy.

116
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:40,460
The likes of a Theodore Roosevelt and the likes of a George Washington and the likes of an Abraham Lincoln.

117
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:45,920
Those were his ideas of the model that an American boy should follow.

118
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:53,080
And it's interesting to me that the movement that we have today comes from these kind of disparate influences.

119
00:10:53,960 --> 00:11:02,660
And you can't call one of them right and one of them wrong, but they all came together to form a scouting movement that's been pretty durable for the past hundred years.

120
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:10,760
Well, if you look at the contributions, you would have to think that Dan Beard's patriotism, that's infused in the Boy Scouts Amendment.

121
00:11:11,959 --> 00:11:18,939
Baden-Pol, looking back to his knights, that was heroics and heroism.

122
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:21,160
That is built in the Boy Scouts.

123
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:22,020
You look at Seton.

124
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:26,780
You look at his vision of the Great Spirit.

125
00:11:26,780 --> 00:11:28,100
That was just one.

126
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:37,980
You look at Christianity and Judaism, and you see that multi-religious faiths are built in the Boy Scout model.

127
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:39,680
I mean so they're all in there.

128
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:47,800
There's not one particular model that completely dominates, completely dominates.

129
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:53,540
BP certainly has the bulk of the system, but it doesn't completely dominate.

130
00:11:53,540 --> 00:11:57,180
I mean you look at the Order of the Arrow, well, that goes back to Seton.

131
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:00,980
And the Order of the Arrow is a huge program in the Boy Scouts of America.

132
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:07,579
So again, you can't say that one completely dominates, but there are certainly major influences by those three individuals.

133
00:12:08,040 --> 00:12:15,780
You know, a lot of histories of scouting that I have read over time take this era, and they kind of encapsulate it in a few paragraphs.

134
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:21,660
While they will mention some of the conflicts and controversies amongst these three men, they kind of downplay it.

135
00:12:21,660 --> 00:12:25,060
What I really enjoy about your work, David, is you just go straight at it.

136
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:30,020
You don't downplay it or anything to kind of celebrate the fact that they were able to work together.

137
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:34,600
It was the fact of what they were able to contribute to each other, whether they liked it or not.

138
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:36,160
And that's the good thing about it.

139
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:38,740
The accomplishments speak for themselves.

140
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:42,400
And how long do you think you spent working on this thing?

141
00:12:42,579 --> 00:12:45,480
Well, I tag it to about 10 years, writing about 8 to 10.

142
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:48,959
For my lights, it was well worth it for the product.

143
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:51,220
Well, thank you very much.

144
00:12:51,320 --> 00:12:53,220
I know my wife would like to hear that.

145
00:12:54,300 --> 00:13:08,400
Because there were many a night in the early days of the early 2000s to which I was staying up until 2, 2.30 and 3 in the morning with these piles of papers at the dinner table in my computer.

146
00:13:08,500 --> 00:13:09,500
And I'm just typing away.

147
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:10,460
I'm thinking, this is horrible.

148
00:13:10,680 --> 00:13:12,160
This is terrible.

149
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:16,460
There's nothing going to come out of this besides a big pile of trash.

150
00:13:16,460 --> 00:13:17,800
Eventually, it turned into something.

151
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:18,860
So that was a good thing.

152
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:21,120
There are a couple other major players in here.

153
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:27,720
And one would be a familiar name to most scouters who've studied a little bit about the history of the BSA.

154
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:29,320
And that's West.

155
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:33,700
James West was the first executive secretary.

156
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:39,920
And within a year or so, he, of course, made the title, created the title of chief scout executive.

157
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:44,160
Jim West was an incredibly difficult taskmaster.

158
00:13:44,579 --> 00:13:54,380
And if he saw a messy desk, he would take that cane that he walked around with and he would sweep that desk clean and say, go pick it up, put it in the right spot.

159
00:13:54,819 --> 00:13:56,740
He would have a good time at my house.

160
00:13:56,959 --> 00:13:59,560
He would wear his cane out.

161
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:07,060
Well, I'm sure he wouldn't be very pleased if he saw the 2,400 square feet of warehouse that are loaded up with books over where I write.

162
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:10,719
He comes off as being very businesslike and very motivated.

163
00:14:10,880 --> 00:14:13,780
But also, he was kind of a cold fish, wasn't he?

164
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:15,180
Well, he absolutely was.

165
00:14:15,339 --> 00:14:18,780
His biggest goal was to be successful.

166
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:20,520
We understand that part.

167
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:28,500
But in a personal level, he wanted to be beloved by the boys as were Dan Beard and Ernest Seton.

168
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:30,380
But he did not have those skills.

169
00:14:30,560 --> 00:14:31,620
He was a cold fish.

170
00:14:31,699 --> 00:14:36,400
He did not have much of a personality other than to make people do exactly what he wanted them to do.

171
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:40,260
But he could not get along with the boys to the extent that Beard and Seton were.

172
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:49,700
They were the symbols of American scouting for those years, Seat 45 and Beard for the remaining 25 or so while he was alive.

173
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:55,220
But that's why he received the moniker of Uncle Dan, the beloved uncle of Boy Scouts.

174
00:14:55,920 --> 00:15:00,020
Just as with the other three, without West, we don't have scouting as we have it today.

175
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:07,220
If you didn't have West, you wouldn't have had the last three points of the scout law, brave, clean, and reverent.

176
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:08,540
Those were added by West.

177
00:15:09,200 --> 00:15:09,740
Oh, no kidding.

178
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:10,640
Oh, absolutely, yes.

179
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:17,840
They took the original nine points, soon to be ten points under BP, and they added brave, clean, and reverent.

180
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:22,660
And that was done at the direction of Jim West.

181
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:29,740
Another big influence and big personality that weaves its way through your narrative in the book is Boyce.

182
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:36,240
Well, William D. Boyce, William Dixon Boyce, was a publisher in Chicago.

183
00:15:36,240 --> 00:15:39,480
He was in England in December of 1909.

184
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:43,560
And it is at that point that the Boy Scout story began.

185
00:15:43,920 --> 00:15:57,600
And that's actually when, according to the information I was able to dig up, that was the time period to which he was escorted across the street by the unknown Boy Scout.

186
00:15:57,600 --> 00:16:02,260
Not if it happened, but it's plausible, and the details are listed in the book.

187
00:16:02,380 --> 00:16:13,200
It's kind of a story that's been around, kind of a legend of the Boy Scouts of America, and it's been kind of controversial as to whether it actually happened or not.

188
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:15,160
But your conclusion was?

189
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:21,500
My conclusion is I would hate for facts to get in the way of a good story, but it is in fact plausible.

190
00:16:22,060 --> 00:16:22,400
Okay.

191
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:29,240
It is plausible based upon the dates and the one day of fog that existed in his area in 1909 in December.

192
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:41,960
It would have given him just enough time to go to London, be in London, go to the scout office, and take off for Southampton on the steamer going back to the United States the next day.

193
00:16:42,060 --> 00:16:44,640
That kind of helps set that to rest, doesn't it?

194
00:16:44,760 --> 00:16:45,180
I mean…

195
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:48,800
Yeah, I was skeptical as well.

196
00:16:49,180 --> 00:16:56,699
There's not enough facts to say it didn't happen, and if you can't prove it didn't happen, you can't deny that it did, to a certain extent.

197
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,819
So it's just one of those things that you have to look at as saying, yeah, it's possible.

198
00:17:01,819 --> 00:17:11,619
These three, Beard and Powell and Seton, make conflicting claims as to who started what and when.

199
00:17:11,619 --> 00:17:19,760
What's the real conclusion that you find about some of these conflicting claims and things like that?

200
00:17:20,319 --> 00:17:26,879
Well, if you look at the timeline, Seton would have based his Woodcraft Indians organization from 1902, and it was based upon Indian lore.

201
00:17:26,879 --> 00:17:36,040
Well, now you have Dan Beard dating his organization, the original sons of Daniel Boone, and the boy pioneers date back to 1905.

202
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:41,460
Well, then you work on to – BP should theoretically date his organization.

203
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:43,080
You can pick two dates.

204
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:51,360
You can pick Brown Sea in July and August of 07 or the actual publication that I do of Scouting for Boys in January of 1908.

205
00:17:51,879 --> 00:17:55,000
And then the BSA, of course, 1910, February.

206
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:04,240
So, I mean, there's several things that you can look at Seton as being the originator of, but BP changed the names and called it something else.

207
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:13,000
And so he – his story actually – his organization actually lasted longer and was more powerful and more interesting to other people.

208
00:18:13,100 --> 00:18:14,060
Therefore, he gets all the credit.

209
00:18:15,020 --> 00:18:21,920
In the contest of ideas, a lot of this we would put right in Seton's lap as being the originator of a lot of these ideas.

210
00:18:21,920 --> 00:18:29,880
I would say that he has a legitimate claim on the origination of certain aspects of the program.

211
00:18:30,580 --> 00:18:36,940
Actually, for the rest of Seton's life, he always claimed that Baden-Pol plagiarized him, and that wasn't exactly the case.

212
00:18:37,960 --> 00:18:47,080
But that was the lifelong hatred that he had for BP, and it never really went away, even in the latest years of their lives.

213
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:48,980
But BP always blew it off.

214
00:18:48,980 --> 00:18:50,840
But they never made it public.

215
00:18:51,040 --> 00:18:53,980
I mean, Seton never came out public and said, you know, BP, you're a plagiarist.

216
00:18:54,060 --> 00:19:00,680
But he did say in his personal letters saying, you know, the guy plagiarized me, and so-and-so said I was the originator of the scouting idea.

217
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:02,380
I can't believe that other people don't see this.

218
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:06,560
But he never came out and called it a problem.

219
00:19:06,700 --> 00:19:10,520
He just sort of sat and stewed in his own little world.

220
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:28,180
It seems to me the real message and the real miracle behind this history is that even though, you know, these guys had their back and forth and their problems, they were all champions of a single set of ideas that really has become what we enjoy today.

221
00:19:28,180 --> 00:19:38,520
Well, it was for the boys, and everything was for the boys, and that you couldn't have a strong military and you couldn't have a strong citizenry if you didn't have strong character.

222
00:19:38,520 --> 00:19:57,400
The growth of the youth movement and youth education and patriots and good citizens was long underway when BP was creating his scouting movement because that's exactly what Seton and Beard were setting off to do earlier in that decade.

223
00:19:57,400 --> 00:20:01,160
This is the way that you conclude the book, if you'll allow me to read it.

224
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:33,540
It's a very modest conclusion to this, David, and I'm wondering if you'd like to expand on it for us.

225
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:45,120
Well, I didn't write the book to be a dissertation upon how the Boy Scouts of America have succeeded or failed in recent years in their program.

226
00:20:45,400 --> 00:20:49,120
That is for the reader to decide whether the Boy Scouts have been successful or not.

227
00:20:49,120 --> 00:20:57,620
But what we can say is in order for the Boy Scouts of America to be successful in the next 100 years, they have to appeal to the boys that live today.

228
00:20:58,479 --> 00:21:05,399
And I've met a number of boys who would just forego advancements for simply being out in the woods.

229
00:21:05,540 --> 00:21:10,360
And that old adage goes that scouting is three-quarters outing, and that's exactly what it is.

230
00:21:10,760 --> 00:21:12,719
A lot of boys just don't care to advance.

231
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:15,020
They just want to get out and be boys.

232
00:21:15,020 --> 00:21:15,580
You know what?

233
00:21:15,639 --> 00:21:17,820
That's the same as it was back in 1910.

234
00:21:17,820 --> 00:21:19,340
Back in 1902.

235
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:20,800
Back in 1905.

236
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:23,679
That's what boys want to do.

237
00:21:23,840 --> 00:21:24,659
They want to be out.

238
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:25,639
They want to be free.

239
00:21:25,800 --> 00:21:26,800
They want to be running around.

240
00:21:26,899 --> 00:21:33,060
They want to have some focus on what to accomplish, whether that's through games or whatever.

241
00:21:33,260 --> 00:21:34,560
But they want to have fun.

242
00:21:34,639 --> 00:21:35,200
F-U-N.

243
00:21:35,300 --> 00:21:36,620
Capital F-U-N.

244
00:21:37,060 --> 00:21:43,100
And they can do that through scouting, and the Boy Scouts have to appeal to those types of boys in order to survive.

245
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:52,159
And I suggest that going back to the woods and staying in the woods and making sure that you have enough scout camps and activities at scout camps, that's the way that you're going to help grow.

246
00:21:52,560 --> 00:22:02,959
Now, it doesn't mean that you're not going to keep creating advancements like the video games activity pen for the Cub Scouts because that's part of life today as well.

247
00:22:02,960 --> 00:22:08,940
So you have to remain focused upon what boys want to see.

248
00:22:09,040 --> 00:22:12,860
And I think it's good marketing if they do do something like that, and they have.

249
00:22:13,300 --> 00:22:20,120
So they have to focus as well on the outdoors and the camping activities, and a number of council districts are doing exactly that.

250
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:32,879
I think what's really remarkable is that this, as you just said, is just as attractive and exciting and engaging to boys now as it was 100 years ago.

251
00:22:33,100 --> 00:22:33,899
Boys are the same.

252
00:22:34,260 --> 00:22:36,100
They just have new toys.

253
00:22:36,479 --> 00:22:45,500
Every once in a while, I hear somebody my age talk about how good things used to be and how things have changed and things are either easier or harder or something like that.

254
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:53,540
But when you get right down to it, the things that appealed to boys in 1910 about scouting are the same things that appeal to them today.

255
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:54,180
Absolutely.

256
00:22:54,800 --> 00:22:57,380
Being outside, messing around, having a good time.

257
00:22:58,140 --> 00:23:01,720
And whether they like it or not, they're probably going to get some training in citizenship.

258
00:23:02,580 --> 00:23:09,080
The book is due to be published at the end of this month, of June of 2010.

259
00:23:09,180 --> 00:23:12,520
It will be released right around there, right around June 30th.

260
00:23:12,520 --> 00:23:13,780
It's at the press right now.

261
00:23:13,780 --> 00:23:20,860
And if I want to get a copy of this right now, what's the best way for me to preorder?

262
00:23:20,920 --> 00:23:26,860
Well, the best way is to go to Amazon.com and go to the Scouting Party's book page and preorder there.

263
00:23:27,280 --> 00:23:27,600
Okay.

264
00:23:27,720 --> 00:23:31,180
And I'm going to make sure to have a link to that for everybody on the blog.

265
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:38,160
David, I really appreciate the book, and I think you've done a great service to us, shining a light on this period of scouting.

266
00:23:38,360 --> 00:23:40,040
And there's many lessons to be learned.

267
00:23:40,100 --> 00:23:41,520
I really encourage people to read it.

268
00:23:41,520 --> 00:23:42,280
Well, thank you, Clark.

269
00:23:42,360 --> 00:23:43,660
I really appreciate that.

270
00:23:43,780 --> 00:23:48,379
It's a no-holds-barred view at what actually happened.

271
00:23:48,740 --> 00:23:53,760
And it's a fascinating story about these fascinating men who create a fascinating organization.

272
00:23:53,920 --> 00:23:56,879
I really appreciate your time and talk to us on the Scoutmaster podcast.

273
00:23:57,340 --> 00:23:59,300
Well, thank you for your kind words.

274
00:23:59,300 --> 00:24:26,940
Well, I really enjoyed talking with David, and I hope you enjoyed listening to the interview.

275
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:28,820
I also enjoyed reading the book.

276
00:24:29,040 --> 00:24:34,120
And I really want to suggest that it would be a great read for you if you're a Scout leader.

277
00:24:34,240 --> 00:24:34,980
And here's why.

278
00:24:35,200 --> 00:24:37,540
I'll reiterate something I said during the interview.

279
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:44,500
At one point within the next week or the next month, if you're an active Scouter, you're going to end up in a committee meeting somewhere.

280
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:49,500
It's going to be a troop committee meeting or a district committee meeting or a council committee meeting.

281
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:52,620
Maybe you're on the committee for a campery or something like that.

282
00:24:52,680 --> 00:24:56,840
Hey, we're all going to be working with other Scouters and volunteers.

283
00:24:57,420 --> 00:25:02,600
And we're going to find some of them to be a little acerbic in their manner.

284
00:25:02,600 --> 00:25:06,459
And we're going to find some of them to have ideas that we differ with.

285
00:25:07,300 --> 00:25:18,620
But the fact that Baden Powell and Ernest Thompson-Seaton and Daniel Carter Beard, even though they really didn't like each other, were able to work together from a distance.

286
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:30,080
And to use their ideas together to form the movement that we have today should be a real lesson to us in the way that we conduct ourselves when we're working along with other volunteers.

287
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:32,800
Now, I am very opinionated.

288
00:25:33,300 --> 00:25:40,500
I'm so opinionated that I have a podcast and I'm sitting here talking into a microphone because I think that I have some pretty good ideas.

289
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:56,679
There are other people who are going to have ideas that might not jive with mine, but in that dynamic tension of ideas may help us keep us lively and centered on the things that really make Scouting what it is.

290
00:25:56,680 --> 00:26:02,360
In my part of the world, we have a very active and vibrant Amish community.

291
00:26:02,900 --> 00:26:06,220
And now, if you're not familiar with the Amish, they live a very simple lifestyle.

292
00:26:06,680 --> 00:26:11,060
So their culture is a bit removed from the culture that I experience.

293
00:26:11,200 --> 00:26:15,820
But I get to go and speak with them and talk to them at our farmer's market.

294
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:22,980
And it's always interesting to talk to somebody with a different set of views or a different take on things.

295
00:26:22,980 --> 00:26:29,320
And I was talking to one of the fellows the other day and he said, some people lean left and some people lean right.

296
00:26:29,440 --> 00:26:34,300
And we need the both of them because if we didn't have that, we'd all fall over.

297
00:26:35,020 --> 00:26:40,400
You know, we need to have a little bit of that dynamic tension built into every part of our lives.

298
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:48,220
Within the next several months or the next several days or the next several weeks, you are going to be sitting in a committee meeting somewhere.

299
00:26:48,960 --> 00:26:51,680
It's going to be a troop pack crew committee.

300
00:26:51,960 --> 00:26:53,660
It's going to be a district committee.

301
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:55,160
It's going to be a council committee.

302
00:26:55,340 --> 00:26:58,480
Maybe you're working on putting a campery together or something like that.

303
00:26:58,660 --> 00:27:02,440
And there's going to be somebody there who you just don't completely agree with.

304
00:27:02,440 --> 00:27:04,560
And that is okay.

305
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:06,260
That's fine.

306
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:19,860
Because if Daniel Carter Beard and Ernest Thompson Seton and Robert Baden-Powell could somehow get along just long enough so that their ideas formed the Boy Scouts of America,

307
00:27:20,060 --> 00:27:25,620
that's a great demonstration of how this dynamic tension can create something good.

308
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:35,439
So when we run across contrary ideas and things like that, it's best to examine them and to try and examine them without judgment,

309
00:27:36,280 --> 00:27:38,820
without, you know, trying to judge whether they're right or wrong,

310
00:27:38,820 --> 00:27:47,979
and to see if we can see something in them that brings the dynamic tension of creation to our own ideas.

311
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:53,260
And it'll make being in a committee meeting a lot more interesting.

312
00:27:55,620 --> 00:27:57,879
Which is really difficult sometimes.

313
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:01,679
But, hey, I hope you'll read the book.

314
00:28:02,280 --> 00:28:05,679
And as I said, I hope you enjoyed listening to the interview.

